        {
          "id": "b-6uc3yn",
          "type": "text",
          "html": "<p></p><div><b><span class=\"big\">In Short</span></b></div><div><ul><li><b>What I Owned: </b>The concept, the core systems (cards, economy, mana, combat), all 40+ cards and the board, and the backlog and roadmap for a 15-person team.</li><li><b>Hardest Problem: </b>Fog of War, it came difficult to balance the rule of the fog of war with the amount of different behavior from the cards.</li><li><b>What it Shows: </b>Leading a project from idea to release, and an economy built to force a real choice every turn.</li></ul></div><p></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "b-68q6iw",
          "type": "spacer",
          "size": "md"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-2hcufq",
      "type": "section",
      "background": "none",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "b-ztgply",
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Design Pillars"
        },
        {
          "id": "b-s2gfzb",
          "type": "text",
          "html": "<p>1. <b>Every turn is a trade-off</b>. One resource for everything means no free actions.</p><p>2. <b>Readable at a glance</b>. Players understand a card's role from its type and layout.</p><p>3. <b>Easy to learn, deep to master</b>.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "b-5fexab",
          "type": "spacer",
          "size": "sm"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-z4ytuv",
      "type": "section",
      "background": "none",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "b-jja6yy",
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "1. One Pool of Mana for Everything"
        },
        {
          "id": "b-eq4yuu",
          "type": "text",
          "html": "<p>Many card games give separate resources for different actions. Here, moving, attacking and defending all draw from the same mana pool, so every turn is a trade-off: spending to advance leaves less to protect yourself.</p>"
        },
